MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Mike,

You are correct with respect to the ranging behavior.

Yes, masking the CDF is an easy, local correction that works fairly well, considering the existing alternatives.

Which method were you using? Now that I think about it again, I think that Tom's original idea of rasterizing the polygon will be faster if the polygon-rasterization algorithm is efficient (but I don't know if raster's is). Tom's code could probably be sped up by precomputing is.na <http://is.na>(raster::as.matrix(rr_re) called in the loop. But he also said that he never got that approach to line up correctly. If that's not faster (and correct), then I will get to coding something that will be fast and correct within the year, as this is a project currently in the pipeline. I think the point-in-polygon approach will be fundamentally slower, as the computation time scales with the product of the number of locations and vertices.

Yup, simple masking will not accurately describe motion around boundaries. The boundary effect has to be applied at the level of the diffusion model to fully correct for that. A more appropriate model would have the probability density diffuse only partially around the boundary, as time & motion permits. I will not have this kind of model in ctmm for some time.

Best,
Chris

